            answer: 'Monopoly power exists when a single firm or a few firms dominate a market, allowing them to influence prices and output. This causes market failure because:\n\n1. Higher Prices: Monopolies can charge prices above marginal cost, unlike competitive markets where price equals marginal cost. Consumers pay more and consumer surplus is reduced.\n\n2. Lower Output: Monopolies restrict output below the competitive level to maintain high prices, leading to underproduction relative to the socially optimal quantity.\n\n3. Allocative Inefficiency: Price exceeds marginal cost (P > MC), meaning the value consumers place on additional units exceeds the cost of production. Society would benefit from more production.\n\n4. Productive Inefficiency: Without competitive pressure, monopolies may not minimize costs or operate at minimum average cost.\n\n5. Reduced Consumer Choice: Limited competition means fewer product varieties and less innovation.\n\n6. Deadweight Loss: The reduction in output creates welfare loss - transactions that would benefit both buyers and sellers don\'t occur.',
