            question: 'How can the government correct market failure through subsidies?',
            answer: 'Government can correct market failure through subsidies by:\n\n1. Subsidizing Merit Goods: Providing financial assistance for goods with positive externalities (education, healthcare, public transport) to lower prices and increase consumption.\n\n2. Internalizing External Benefits: Subsidies compensate for external benefits not reflected in market price, encouraging optimal production and consumption.\n\n3. Shifting Supply Curve: Subsidies shift the supply curve rightward, increasing quantity to the socially optimal level where marginal social benefit equals marginal social cost.\n\n4. Lower Prices: Reduced prices make merit goods more affordable and accessible to all income groups.\n\n5. Examples in Singapore:\n- MRT subsidies to encourage public transport usage and reduce road congestion\n- Healthcare subsidies at polyclinics and public hospitals\n- Education subsidies for schools and universities\n- HDB housing subsidies to make homes affordable\n- GST vouchers for lower-income households\n\n6. Benefit: Society gains from increased consumption of beneficial goods and services.',
