            question: 'How can the government correct market failure through regulation?',
            answer: 'Government can correct market failure through regulation by:\n\n1. Legal Controls: Setting rules and standards that producers must follow. For example, emission standards for factories, safety standards for products, and quality requirements.\n\n2. Bans and Restrictions: Prohibiting or limiting harmful activities. Examples include banning smoking in public places, restricting sale of certain goods, or limiting business hours.\n\n3. Licensing: Requiring permits or licenses to ensure only qualified producers operate. This maintains quality standards and protects consumers.\n\n4. Direct Quantity Control: Setting maximum limits on negative externalities (pollution quotas) or minimum requirements for positive externalities.\n\n5. Singapore Examples:\n- NEA regulations on factory emissions and waste disposal\n- Smoking bans in most public areas\n- Vehicle emission standards and inspections\n- Building regulations and safety codes\n- Food safety regulations for hawker centres and restaurants\n\n6. Enforcement: Fines and penalties for violations ensure compliance with regulations.\n\n7. Advantages: Provides certainty, can completely eliminate harmful practices, and directly addresses the problem.',
