            question: 'How can the government correct market failure through provision of public goods?',
            answer: 'Government can correct market failure by directly providing public goods that the free market fails to supply:\n\n1. Direct Provision: Government uses tax revenue to produce and provide public goods for free or at subsidized rates to overcome the free-rider problem.\n\n2. Overcoming Market Failure: Since public goods are non-excludable and non-rival, private firms cannot profit from providing them. Government provision ensures these socially beneficial goods are available.\n\n3. Universal Access: Everyone can access public goods regardless of ability to pay, ensuring equity and social welfare.\n\n4. Singapore Examples:\n- National defence (Singapore Armed Forces)\n- Street lighting throughout Singapore\n- Public parks and Gardens by the Bay\n- Public roads and infrastructure\n- Police and emergency services\n- Public beaches and waterfronts\n- National monuments and heritage sites\n\n5. Funding: These goods are funded through general taxation, spreading costs across all taxpayers.\n\n6. Social Benefit: Society enjoys goods that would not exist under a pure market system, improving overall welfare.',
