            question: 'What is Maximum Price Control and what are its effects?',
            answer: 'Maximum price control (price ceiling) is a government-imposed limit on how high a price can be charged for a good or service, set below the market equilibrium price to make essential goods affordable.\n\nEffects:\n\n1. Shortage: Quantity demanded exceeds quantity supplied at the controlled price. Consumers want more than producers are willing to supply.\n\n2. Increased Demand: Lower price encourages more consumption than at equilibrium.\n\n3. Decreased Supply: Producers reduce supply as lower price reduces profitability.\n\n4. Rationing Problem: Not all consumers who want the good can obtain it, creating need for allocation systems (queuing, coupons).\n\n5. Black Market: Illegal markets may emerge where goods are sold above the legal maximum price.\n\n6. Quality Deterioration: Producers may reduce quality to maintain profitability.\n\n7. Example: If Singapore imposed maximum rent controls, landlords would supply fewer rental units while more people would seek to rent, creating housing shortage.',
