            question: 'What is Minimum Price Control and what are its effects? Give a wage example.',
            answer: 'Minimum price control (price floor) is a government-imposed limit on how low a price can fall, set above the market equilibrium price to protect producers or workers.\n\nEffects:\n\n1. Surplus: Quantity supplied exceeds quantity demanded at the controlled price. Producers want to supply more than consumers are willing to buy.\n\n2. Decreased Demand: Higher price discourages consumption.\n\n3. Increased Supply: Producers increase supply as higher price increases profitability.\n\n4. Inefficient Allocation: Resources are wasted producing goods that cannot be sold.\n\nMinimum Wage Example:\nGovernment sets minimum wage at $10/hour when equilibrium wage is $8/hour.\n\nEffects:\n- More workers seek employment at $10/hour (increased labor supply)\n- Employers demand fewer workers at $10/hour (decreased labor demand)\n- Unemployment results as labor supply exceeds demand\n- Helps workers who keep jobs (earn $10 instead of $8)\n- Harms workers who lose jobs or cannot find employment\n\nNote: Singapore has sector-specific Progressive Wage Model rather than universal minimum wage, covering cleaners, security officers, and landscaping workers.',
