            question: 'What are Indirect Taxes and the difference between Specific and Ad Valorem taxes?',
            answer: 'Indirect taxes are taxes on goods and services imposed on producers but passed on to consumers through higher prices. They are used to discourage consumption of demerit goods and raise government revenue.\n\nSpecific Tax:\n- Fixed amount per unit (e.g., $2 per pack of cigarettes)\n- Tax amount does not change with price\n- Causes parallel leftward shift of supply curve (vertical distance = tax amount)\n- Examples: Singapore cigarette duty (specific amount per stick), alcohol duty\n\nAd Valorem Tax:\n- Percentage of the price (e.g., 7% GST)\n- Tax amount increases with price\n- Causes non-parallel leftward shift of supply curve (rotates inward)\n- Gap between original and new supply curves widens as price increases\n- Examples: Singapore GST (7%), import duties (percentage of value)\n\nBoth types:\n- Increase price paid by consumers\n- Decrease quantity demanded\n- Create tax burden shared between consumers and producers\n- Can be used to correct negative externalities\n\nSingapore uses both types to discourage harmful consumption and raise revenue.',
