            question: 'Explain Tax Incidence and how PED and PES affect who bears the tax burden.',
            answer: 'Tax incidence refers to how the burden of a tax is divided between consumers and producers. The distribution depends on price elasticity of demand (PED) and supply (PES).\n\nWhen Demand is Inelastic (PED < 1):\n- Consumers bear most of tax burden\n- Consumers are relatively unresponsive to price changes, so they continue buying despite higher prices\n- Producers can pass most of tax to consumers\n- Example: Cigarettes have inelastic demand, so smokers pay most of cigarette tax\n\nWhen Demand is Elastic (PED > 1):\n- Producers bear most of tax burden\n- Consumers are very responsive to price changes and reduce consumption significantly\n- Producers must absorb more tax to avoid losing customers\n- Example: Luxury goods with many substitutes\n\nWhen Supply is Inelastic (PES < 1):\n- Producers bear most of tax burden\n- Producers cannot easily reduce supply, so they absorb more tax\n- Example: Agricultural products with fixed growing seasons\n\nWhen Supply is Elastic (PES > 1):\n- Consumers bear most of tax burden\n- Producers can easily adjust supply and pass tax to consumers\n\nKey Principle: The side of the market that is more inelastic bears more of the tax burden.',
