            question: 'What are Subsidies and who benefits from them?',
            answer: 'Subsidies are payments from the government to producers to reduce production costs and lower prices. They encourage production and consumption of merit goods with positive externalities.\n\nHow Subsidies Work:\n- Government pays producers a fixed amount per unit or percentage of costs\n- Shifts supply curve rightward (increases supply)\n- Lowers market price\n- Increases quantity traded\n\nWho Benefits (Subsidy Incidence):\n\n1. Consumers: Pay lower prices and consume more\n- Benefit more when demand is elastic (price-sensitive)\n- Gain more consumer surplus\n\n2. Producers: Receive subsidy payment and may charge slightly higher price than before subsidy\n- Benefit more when supply is elastic\n- Gain more producer surplus\n\nSingapore Examples:\n- MRT subsidies: Commuters pay lower fares, transport operators receive subsidy\n- Healthcare subsidies: Patients pay less at polyclinics/public hospitals, hospitals receive government funding\n- HDB subsidies: Homebuyers pay below market price, HDB receives subsidy\n- Education subsidies: Students pay lower fees, schools receive government funding\n- Hawker centre subsidies: Diners pay affordable prices, hawkers receive rental subsidies\n\nThe more inelastic the demand, the more consumers benefit from subsidies.',
