            question: 'What is Deadweight Loss from government intervention?',
            answer: 'Deadweight loss (welfare loss) is the loss of economic efficiency and social welfare that occurs when government intervention prevents markets from reaching equilibrium.\n\nCauses of Deadweight Loss:\n\n1. From Taxes:\n- Tax increases price and reduces quantity below equilibrium\n- Some mutually beneficial transactions don\'t occur\n- Both consumer and producer surplus decrease\n- The reduction in surplus exceeds tax revenue collected\n- Area between supply and demand curves represents welfare loss\n\n2. From Subsidies:\n- Subsidies lower price and increase quantity above equilibrium\n- Encourages production where marginal cost exceeds marginal benefit\n- Resources are overallocated to subsidized goods\n- Opportunity cost: resources could be better used elsewhere\n\n3. From Price Controls:\n- Maximum price creates shortage - welfare loss from unmet demand\n- Minimum price creates surplus - welfare loss from unsold goods\n\nExample: If Singapore heavily taxes rice, consumption falls significantly. The welfare loss includes:\n- Consumers who would have bought rice at equilibrium but don\'t at taxed price\n- Producers who would have sold rice at equilibrium but don\'t at reduced quantity\n\nHowever, deadweight loss may be acceptable if intervention achieves important social objectives like reducing negative externalities or promoting merit goods.',
