            question: 'What is Privatisation and what are its advantages and disadvantages?',
            answer: 'Privatisation is the transfer of ownership of state-owned enterprises to the private sector. Government sells public assets or businesses to private investors.\n\nAdvantages:\n\n1. Increased Efficiency: Profit motive drives cost reduction and productivity improvements\n2. Better Management: Private sector expertise and competitive pressure improve operations\n3. Innovation: Competition encourages development of new products and services\n4. Reduced Government Burden: Government no longer needs to fund losses or provide subsidies\n5. Revenue Generation: Sale of assets provides one-time revenue for government\n6. Consumer Choice: Competition provides more options for consumers\n\nDisadvantages:\n\n1. Loss of Government Control: Government loses ability to direct operations for social objectives\n2. Higher Prices: Private firms may charge higher prices to maximize profit\n3. Reduced Access: Essential services may become unaffordable for low-income groups\n4. Job Losses: Private owners may cut jobs to reduce costs\n5. Natural Monopolies: Some industries (utilities) may become private monopolies exploiting consumers\n6. Short-term Focus: Private firms may prioritize short-term profits over long-term investment\n\nSingapore Example: SingTel was privatised in 1993, introducing competition and innovation in telecommunications, but government retained Golden Share to ensure national interests.',
