            question: 'What is Nationalisation and what are its advantages and disadvantages?',
            answer: 'Nationalisation is the transfer of ownership of private businesses to the government. The state takes control of industries or companies, typically those providing essential services.\n\nAdvantages:\n\n1. Government Control: Ensures essential services are provided to all citizens\n2. Social Welfare: Decisions can prioritize social objectives over profit\n3. Prevent Exploitation: Stops private monopolies from charging excessive prices\n4. Long-term Planning: Government can invest in infrastructure and services for future benefit\n5. Universal Access: Ensures everyone can access essential services regardless of income\n6. Accountability: Public ownership means democratic oversight and accountability\n\nDisadvantages:\n\n1. Inefficiency: Lack of profit motive may lead to waste and poor management\n2. Lack of Competition: Absence of competitive pressure reduces innovation\n3. Government Burden: Taxpayers bear costs of losses and inefficiencies\n4. Political Interference: Decisions may be influenced by politics rather than economics\n5. Slow Response: Bureaucracy may prevent quick adaptation to changing market conditions\n6. Reduced Consumer Choice: Monopoly provision limits options for consumers\n\nSingapore Context: Rather than full nationalisation, Singapore uses Government-Linked Companies (GLCs) like SMRT and government agencies like HDB to provide essential services while maintaining some market mechanisms.',
