            question: 'Compare the effectiveness of Regulation vs Taxation in correcting market failure.',
            answer: 'Effectiveness comparison of regulation and taxation:\n\nRegulation:\n\nAdvantages:\n- Provides certainty - specific limits or bans ensure compliance\n- Can completely eliminate harmful practices\n- Easier to enforce through inspections and penalties\n- More direct approach to addressing specific problems\n- Example: Singapore NEA pollution limits ensure factories don\'t exceed emissions\n\nDisadvantages:\n- Inflexible - same rules apply to all firms regardless of circumstances\n- High enforcement costs (monitoring, inspections, legal proceedings)\n- May stifle innovation and business growth\n- Difficult to set optimal standards\n- Firms may lobby for weaker regulations\n\nTaxation:\n\nAdvantages:\n- Market-based - allows firms flexibility in how to respond\n- Generates government revenue\n- Encourages innovation to reduce tax burden\n- Can be adjusted gradually\n- Example: Singapore carbon tax incentivizes firms to reduce emissions\n\nDisadvantages:\n- Uncertain outcome - depends on price elasticity\n- May be insufficient if demand is inelastic\n- Difficult to calculate optimal tax level\n- Firms may simply pay tax and continue polluting\n- Regressive impact on consumers\n\nWhich is more effective depends on:\n- Severity of the problem (regulation better for serious threats)\n- Need for certainty vs flexibility\n- Enforcement capability\n- Political acceptability\n\nSingapore often combines both: regulations set standards while taxes provide market incentives.',
