            answer: 'Effectiveness comparison of direct provision and subsidies for merit goods:\n\nDirect Provision:\n\nAdvantages:\n- Ensures universal access regardless of income\n- Government maintains full control over quality and standards\n- Can guarantee availability in all areas (no market gaps)\n- Achieves equity objectives\n- Example: Singapore public schools ensure all children access education\n\nDisadvantages:\n- High cost to government (full funding required)\n- May lack efficiency without competitive pressure\n- Potential for bureaucracy and waste\n- Consumers may overuse "free" services\n- Limited consumer choice\n\nSubsidies:\n\nAdvantages:\n- Lower cost to government (partial funding)\n- Maintains private sector involvement and competition\n- Consumers retain choice between providers\n- More efficient as providers compete for customers\n- Example: Singapore subsidizes healthcare at polyclinics while allowing private clinics\n\nDisadvantages:\n- May not ensure universal access if subsidies insufficient\n- Requires means-testing to target support\n- Private providers may still charge unaffordable prices\n- Complex administration\n- May not fully correct underconsumption\n\nWhich is more effective depends on:\n- Importance of universal access\n- Budget constraints\n- Need for consumer choice\n- Extent of underconsumption\n\nSingapore typically uses combination: direct provision for basic services (public schools, polyclinics) with subsidies allowing private alternatives (private schools with government grants, private healthcare with Medisave).',
