            question: 'Evaluate the effectiveness of different government interventions considering unintended consequences.',
            answer: 'Government interventions can have unintended consequences that reduce their effectiveness:\n\n1. Taxes on Demerit Goods:\nIntended: Reduce consumption\nUnintended consequences:\n- Black markets may emerge (illegal cigarettes)\n- Consumers may switch to other harmful substitutes\n- Regressive impact on poor (spend larger share of income on taxed goods)\n- May be ineffective if demand highly inelastic\n\n2. Subsidies on Merit Goods:\nIntended: Increase consumption\nUnintended consequences:\n- Overconsumption and waste (e.g., overuse of subsidized healthcare)\n- Producers become dependent on subsidies\n- Difficult to remove politically\n- Opportunity cost of government spending\n\n3. Price Controls:\nIntended: Make goods affordable (max price) or protect producers (min price)\nUnintended consequences:\n- Shortages or surpluses\n- Black markets\n- Quality deterioration\n- Reduced investment in the industry\n\n4. Regulations:\nIntended: Eliminate harmful practices\nUnintended consequences:\n- High compliance costs for businesses\n- May drive activities underground\n- Can stifle innovation and competition\n- Enforcement challenges\n\nEvaluation Criteria:\n- Achievement of objectives vs unintended effects\n- Cost to government and economy\n- Time taken to see results\n- Political and social acceptability\n- Sustainability and long-term impacts\n\nSingapore approach: Carefully designed mix of interventions with regular evaluation and adjustment to minimize unintended consequences while achieving policy objectives.',
